### The parent wants to know when daycare crying will ease and how to help the child settle.

18 of 98 posts.

Children range from 14 weeks to about 20 months, with some older toddlers’ exact ages unstated in their quotes. Parents describe crying at the door, throughout care or on reunion, refusal to eat or play, distress after a move, and distress returning after an initially easy start. They ask for timelines, reassurance and practical changes; one considers pausing care versus increasing attendance to five days.

- "It's been 3 weeks of 3 days per week and they just emailed me to basically say that she's not adjusting. She often refuses to eat and doesn't play or participate. […]" [sr072-0119]
- "But after a couple of weeks, things completely changed. For the last few days, she’s been crying almost the whole time, starting right at drop-off. […]" [sr101-0023]
- "[…] I have never let him cry more than a couple minutes in his life; I thought I could never do CIO sleep training because I hate to hear him cry, and then I’ve effectively left him to cry 3h straight with no drinks and food in between?" [sr115-0061]
- "[…] She goes with her favourite stuffy and her soother. I sent her favourite snacks and her favourite fruits and she won’t take anything." [sr134-0046]
- "Basically just wondering if it’s his temperament or if he just needs more time." [sr020-0154]
- "[…] I can't help but feel like I've done something wrong, even though I know I probably haven't. […]" [sr072-0004]

**But:** Drop-off crying does not always continue into the day: "Nursery says he doesn't cry long and quickly engages in play and all is well. […]" [sr113-0032] Another child settled within minutes: "[…] They called me an hour later to say she only cried for a few minutes." [sr025-0105]

