### The parent asks for a slower daycare introduction because abrupt separation feels wrong.

2 of 98 posts.

Two parents of a 13-month-old and a ten-month-old question an introduction that moves quickly to separation or full days. They want accompanied visits and time to build familiarity with a caregiver before leaving. One negotiates shorter separations; the other later withdraws the child, also citing food allergies.

- "[…] What could it possibly hurt to let us visit together first and give him a little time to get familiar before being ripped out of my arms?" [sr090-0047]
- "[…] Why do we ask our kids to be comfortable with total strangers instead of getting to know them first?" [sr090-0054]
- "[…] I've been negotiating shorter separations and staying for all sessions so far (although only for a short time), and they've been flexible, but it's clearly not their default. […]" [sr123-0127]
- "I'm making the nursery take it slowly with my little one, (for which i thank them profusely every day) but I feel like somehow I'm in the wrong for taking my baby's input as to how quickly we're doing it." [sr123-0128]

**But:** The quick-start setting did supply moments of play, even though the parent remained concerned: "[…] They sent a picture on day 2 & 3 where he was playing, not crying but not smiling either. […]" [sr090-0046]

