### The parent worries that daycare expects too much independence or leaves the distressed child without enough care.

6 of 98 posts.

Parents question brief soothing, pressure to sleep train, criticism that a child is too needy, and expectations of self-feeding or self-regulation. The posts include a seven-month-old and toddlers of 18–19 months, sometimes following a classroom or daycare change. They describe threats of losing care, two hours of crying, repeated bites and uncertainty about defending the child or changing provision; the two 19-month-old soothing posts appear to be cross-posts.

- "[…] It breaks my heart to see him standing there "unseen" and distressed while the day goes on around him." [sr009-0099]
- "[…] the crying went on for 2 hours before he eventually fell asleep on his own for 20 mins. they are also pushing me to sleep train, which i do not want to do." [sr017-0079]
- "Today, the teacher seemed really fed up and asked, “Does he ever meet other kids? Are there other kids at home? We cannot give him 1:1 care, we have other kids to take care of.”" [sr025-0117]
- "[…] She said that the child who is the biter apparently tries to comfort my daughter when she cries and then eventually gets frustrated and bites her." [sr091-0104]
- "[…] There was not a single time i walked in and felt that I’m comfortable leaving my toddler there. […]" [sr094-0082]

**But:** Parents also recognise the limits of group care, and one explicitly reports comfort: "[…] i don’t expect daycares to hold my baby all day and some crying is to be expected. […]" [sr017-0081] "They have been very patient and supportive and they do comfort her when she needs it […]" [sr091-0107]

