### The parent weighs starting group care for learning and company against keeping the child at home.

7 of 98 posts.

Parents consider voluntary nursery, preschool or Montessori attendance for social contact, learning, preparation for later school and sometimes a few hours of relief. Current children range from babies of roughly seven to 11 months to toddlers approaching two; one eight-month-old’s parent is planning entry at 23 or 30 months. Decisions include timing around a new sibling, illness reaching a newborn, readiness for a one-year-old class, and criticism from another parent.

- "[…] I really want to make sure she is prepared going into Pre-K environments and want to get the ball rolling on some of her social and emotional skills with other peers her age. […]" [sr072-0085]
- "[…] I'm a bit hesitant to do this because I'm nervous about the sudden changes for her and her getting sick all the time and bringing the sickness back to the new baby." [sr040-0056]
- "What’s everybody’s thoughts on “preschool” at 1? It would be Tuesdays and thursdays for 3 hours. Should I ask about maybe enrolling her after Christmas?" [sr050-0083]
- "[…] she sort of belittled and told me I should not do that, and that the kids should be at home till they start school. […]" [sr124-0033]
- "[…] I’m debating if I should wait to have her start in September (at 30 months old) or start in January at 23 months. […]" [sr131-0040]

**But:** One parent questions whether formal early schooling adds anything to home teaching and organised peer contact, drawing on their former nanny work rather than a trial with their own child: "[…] What I was getting at was why put your child into a school like setting if you are able to teach them basic school skills and expose them to social settings and interactions yourself. […]" [sr027-0063]

