### The parent wants childcare that suits the child’s developmental needs and temperament.

8 of 98 posts.

Parents compare centre care, home care, Montessori, language immersion and an available Waldorf-inspired setting against a particular child’s needs. Children range from three to seven months through toddlers of 14–19 months and one described as just two; concerns include stimulation, movement, language, shyness, emerging independence and delayed skills. Some want continuity, while others fear too little teaching, unsuitable equipment or a schedule that forces conformity.

- "What becomes more important as she ages over the next few months and years? Is it consistency in caretakers, flexibility in the daycare’s days/hours, learning/teaching methods, number of teachers, or something totally different?" [sr011-0230]
- "He has been at home with me since birth. My reason for sending him to daycare is to hopefully gain more socialization skills (that one can at that age) and hopefully pick up some language and communication skill." [sr037-0066]
- "[…] Everything is beige. The infant room is pretty much silent. There are no toys. It feels really deprived and depressing, frankly. […]" [sr095-0039]
- "All this to say I worry the day care teachers won’t be able or know how to deal with her? Will she be behind or get in trouble for not being as able as the other kids." [sr090-0132]
- "[…] I guess I’m just concerned that maybe he should go to a regular daycare that has the age groups more segregated (and therefore safer) until he reaches a young toddler age. […]" [sr108-0060]
- "[…] My initial thoughts were to go with a childminder as my daughter is very reserved and introverted, and doesn’t like lots of other people/children so I thought a smaller setting with a childminder but now I’m thinking maybe a nursery would be better? […]" [sr131-0072]

**But:** A seemingly attractive new method need not outweigh a setting that already works: "Before starting at this daycare and seeing how well she’s done, I would have assumed that future me would jump at the opportunity to get her in the Spanish immersion program. But now I think I’d rather keep her where she is. […]" [sr056-0042]

