### The parent questions a settling approach without clear goodbyes or active caregiver engagement.

1 of 63 posts.

This parent questions a settling process lasting 4.5 weeks without visible progress. The caregivers ask the parent to stay until the child sleeps, then sneak away, and to ignore the child while present. The parent wants a recognisable goodbye and reunion and more caregiver initiative; the child’s age is unknown.

- "[…] Instead, they just wait for a convenient moment, usually when our son falls asleep and we're told to sneak out quietly while he's asleep, staying there the whole time until he does." [sr105-0032]
- "[…] There's no "mama/papa goes away for a bit but comes back" moment for him to learn from. […]" [sr105-0033]
- "Their advice was for us to actively ignore our son while we're at daycare, so he learns to rely on the caregivers instead of us and starts trusting them." [sr105-0034]

**But:** This is not a demand for still more time with a parent present; that is already happening. The caregivers give a non-forcing rationale: "[…] they said they don't want to force the relationship, that he should come to them on his own." [sr105-0035]

